llary Clinton the goal wasn't to  do that for Trump this was long before  Trump was the nominee but it was clear  that Clinton was probably going to be  the Democratic candidate and Trump wound  up being the beneficiary of bandhans  efforts the other thing he did is when  he took over Trump's campaign in August  Trump was really floundering in the  polls Bannon managed to get Trump  focused away from megyn kelly away  from the Khan family and use all that  anti-hillary knowledge to keep him  focused on the opposite but he'd already  begun to talk about issues that Bannon  liked in terms of immigration and and a  kind of populism too he had him if you  look at Trump and I do it a chapter on  Trump's political history before been  and he has always had these populist  impulses back in the 80s you could hear  him talking about trade deals and  instead of maligning China  he was maligning Japan so there was a  lot of overlap political overlap between  Trump and ban and to begin with but I  think Bannon really brought the idea of  illegal immigration and understood its  power as a political issue and Trump  really became the vessel for those ideas  and it's what carried him to the white  but Bannon also called him the imperfect  vessel right yes so they don't they're  not together on everything they're not  together on everything I think Bannon  like most of us certainly myself did not  understand from the get-go what a  powerful politician Donald Trump was  going to be Bandon was in advising him  informally as long ago as 2010 thought  he was an interesting guy but nobody  thought that he would win the nomination  or the presidency only when he knocked  out the rest of the Republican field I  think they can powerful politician apart  from banner not anybody else  I think that he has this great personal  force as a candidate I mean we saw in  the Republican primaries Donald Trump  doesn't think a lot about policy but  he's able to kind of dominate his  opponents in a way that most politicians  aren't and and he used a lot of Van  Daan's ideas to do that and to knock out  what everybody thought was the strongest  Republican presidential field in a  generation  I remember watching on Inauguration Day  it was a rainy day if you recall and  once everybody already left including  the president everyone went back into  the Capitol two people stayed at the  podium  Steve Bannon Kellyanne Conway sort of  looking out of the terrace and saying  you know look at what we've accomplished  and  bannon's mind could donald trump have  won the presidency without his aid I  think if you tortured Bannon he wouldn't  answer that question but my answer to  that question is no I don't think Trump  could have for two reasons one that  abandons efforts especially the book  Clinton cash which we  remember from the outset of the campaign  which bandit helped mastermind really  tarnished his opponent in a way that she  never fully recovered from and then on  the flip side his ability to keep Trump  focused on Clinton in the homestretch of  the race and then he had the James Comey  revelations and and suddenly Trump was  able to pull ahead I give Bandhan a lot  of credit for having helped Trump do  that is it possible now that there's  going to be some kind of up split  between Trump base which is which Bannen  also has served well because of the  kinds of things that are happening in  terms of the welfare of that group of  populist Americans you know it's  interesting there haven't been any signs  or they've been very few signs that  Trump is losing his base it's that's  that that's happened he's lost he never  had the support of Democrats he's lost a  lot of the support that he had with  independence but his base has stuck with  him the kind of people that Steve been  in talks to have so far stuck with him  the question is going to be can Trump  ever deliver anything for these people  legislatively or is he gonna spend four  years in mesh and scandal the same time  during the election he also got  independence he got Rust Belt Democrats  I mean there it was a broader audience  than just his base well I count them as  being part of his base but but  ultimately he needs to deliver for them  economically and fulfill some of these  promises he made and there's no sign yet  that he's really gonna be able to do  that in any area other than cracking  down on illegal immigration which of  course was one of trumps big promises we  have to mention we did reach out to the  White House for comments on the  reporting in your book we have not heard  back about what what they think about  all that but Steve banner I mean this is  a guy also who made a lot of money and  has now made a lot of political hay  railing against the élysée and the folks  who have a lot of money I mean I suppose  not dissimilar to Donald Trump in that  way  how does he Square his his his worldview  and sort of how he's developed here  financially you know III think it's  similar in a way to Donald Trump me he  was a guy who was very smart who  succeeded in all sorts of different  realms but came from a blue-collar Navy  family deeply traditional  catholic background managed to get into  Harvard Business School kind of survived  by his own wits and Goldman Sachs and in  Hollywood I think that really gave him a  connection to Trump who saw him as a  deal maker as someone who spoke his  language as somebody who's comfortable  with moguls and as somebody who had  ideas that Trump recognized I think even  just in an intuitive level could advance  Trump's political career they play down  at any tension between him and Jarrett  Kushner's well fascinating book I know  you've wanted to interview him since you  first met him thank you so much thank  you thank you

